<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
    Norwegian salmon farm for the BlueROV2 Heavy, used by AquacultureTest.

    A 400 x 400 m site on the Trondelag coast: three circular pens on a shared mooring grid
    over a 66 m fjord basin, with the fjord wall rising to the shore along the west edge. The
    vehicle is defined in bluerov2_heavy.scn and included unchanged at the bottom of this
    file; the net-inspection payload is added from C++ in AquacultureTestManager (see WHY THE
    PAYLOAD IS NOT HERE, below).

    Frame convention is NED: X forward/north, Y starboard/east, Z DOWN. The water surface is
    z = 0, so depth is POSITIVE z and structures above the waterline have NEGATIVE z.

    ================================ THE SITE ================================
        x in [-200, -192]     shore shelf, 5 m of water
        x in [-192, -116]     the fjord wall, 5 -> 64 m, about 38 degrees
        x in [-116, +200]     the basin, 64 -> 70 m of soft mud

        pens at (0, -70), (0, 0), (0, +70)     hero pen is the middle one
        feed barge at (0, -140)
        vehicle spawn (-21.6, 0, 3)            2.5 m off the hero pen's net, bow on it

    The row of pens runs along y, which is the fjord axis and the direction of the 0.30 m/s
    tidal current. The vehicle spawns due SOUTH of the hero pen because bluerov2_heavy.scn
    hard-codes rpy="0 0 0" on the robot's world transform (line 558) - the vehicle always
    starts heading north, and adding a robot_rpy argument would change a file two other
    demos share. Starting 2.5 m off the net with the bow already on it means NET HOLD can
    engage itself on the first frame.

    ============================ THE PEN, AND HOW IT IS BUILT ============================
    A standard Norwegian plastic pen: a 120 m circumference collar, so 38.2 m across, with a
    cylindrical net wall to 15 m and a conical bottom to 20 m at the axis.

    THE NET IS REAL GEOMETRY, NOT A TEXTURE. Stonefish has no transparency of any kind -
    LookType::TRANSPARENT is declared in OpenGLDataStructs.h and used nowhere, the underwater
    material shader multiplies rgb by albedo.a and then forces a = 1.0
    (materialUUv.frag:154), and CreateLook loads albedo textures with three channels so the
    sampled alpha is always 1 anyway. A netting texture on a solid shell would therefore be an
    opaque wall. The salmon are the point of a fish pen, so the twine is modelled.

    Each object carries TWO meshes: the twine lattice for rendering, and a smooth low-poly
    shell for physics. Without the shell the vehicle flies between the twines and every ray
    the net profiler casts misses through a mesh opening. `<static type="model">` supports the
    split directly (ScenarioParser.cpp:1048-1096) - note that inside `<physical>` and
    `<visual>` the `<origin>` is read as the `<mesh>` element's NEXT SIBLING, so the order of
    those two children is fixed.

    The lattice is coarse and honest about it. Real salmon netting is 25 mm bar with 2.4 mm
    twine, which over 3000 square metres of net is about three million meshes. make_fish_farm.py
    coarsens the pitch and WIDENS THE TWINE TO PRESERVE SOLIDITY instead:

        open fraction = (1 - d/lambda)^2,   Sn = 1 - open fraction = 0.1825 for clean netting

    so the fraction of light the net passes - and therefore how grey it looks at range, which
    is what an inspection actually judges - is exact, while a close-up shows meshes an order
    of magnitude too large. The fouled band carries Sn = 0.35, which makes it visibly denser
    as well as browner, and that is the correct physical signature of fouling.

    Twine cross-section is a TRIANGULAR PRISM, not a flat ribbon. A ribbon lying in the net
    surface has a radial normal, so flying ALONG the net - which is most of an inspection -
    puts every strand edge-on and the net turns transparent exactly when it is being
    inspected. A prism also avoids OpenGLContent::CheckAndRepairFaceVertexOrder, which flips a
    triangle whose geometric normal opposes all three of its vertex normals: a back face built
    by re-winding the same vertices is precisely that case, and the loader silently turns it
    back into a coincident duplicate of the front face.

    The pen is FOUR objects and not sixteen panels. There is no mesh cache in OpenGLContent -
    BuildObject unconditionally creates a new VAO and VBO - and DrawObjects walks the whole
    queue with no frustum culling, once for the main pass, once for the above-water redraw,
    once per shadow cascade and once per spot light. Fewer, fatter objects win, and a merged
    ring is smaller on disk than sixteen panels because it shares vertices across the seams.
    The split that remains is by LOOK and by PHYSICS: a fouled top band, a clean lower band, a
    single torn sector whose shell carries the matching hole, and the cone.

    THE NET IS FROZEN. make_fish_farm.py bakes a steady deformation at the design current and
    the shape does not respond to <current> at runtime. Real nets deform substantially, a
    static mesh cannot, and baking is the honest option. If the current in this file is
    changed, re-run the generator with --current; AquacultureTestManager warns at startup if
    the two disagree.

    ============================= THE WATERLINE =============================
    Structures here DO cross z = 0 - the collar, the handrail, the buoys and the barge all
    have freeboard, because a farm with no freeboard reads as a wreck.

    under_ice.scn states that nothing may sit above z = 0 or the underwater material shader
    blows it out white. That is true of the FIRST pass and not of the finished frame. When the
    eye is above water, OpenGLPipeline draws every object in UNDERWATER mode, blends the ocean
    surface over it weighted by the Fresnel term, and then REDRAWS everything in FULL mode
    with depth testing (OpenGLPipeline.cpp:656-700). The redraw lands on exactly equal
    logarithmic depth values and the global depth function is GL_LEQUAL, so the correct
    above-water shading overwrites the blown-out pass. Submerged fragments are rejected
    because the surface already wrote depth in front of them. From an underwater eye the
    ocean backsurface (OpenGLFlatOcean.cpp:246) is opaque and always nearer than anything
    above the surface, so freeboard is hidden rather than blown out - which is also what a
    diver sees. FloatingTest has floated a hull across the waterline since before this fork.

    Two rules survive:
      - NOTHING MAY BE TANGENT TO z = 0. Every structure gets a definite crossing, so no facet
        lies in the plane where the two regimes meet. The collar tubes are centred at
        z = -0.25 and z = +0.25 with a 0.20 m radius.
      - The vehicle's cameras must stay below the surface. OpenGLPipeline chooses its path
        from the EYE POINT alone, so a camera at z = -0.05 renders the whole scene through
        the above-water path and the picture pops.

    <waves height="0.0"/> follows, but NOT for the reason under_ice.scn gives. With waves the
    water level is a per-pixel FFT displacement oscillating about 0, and a collar that is a
    FIXED body cannot heave with it - the sea would visibly saw through a rigid ring. The wave
    path also runs a compute dispatch per view per frame, where the flat path's UpdateSurface
    is empty. A sheltered fjord at slack water is a fair scene.

    ========================= MATERIALS ARE ALSO SONAR REFLECTIVITY =========================
    OpenGLFLS/SSS/MSIS shade an echo as clamp(dot(N, toEye), 0, 1) * restitution
    (Library/shaders/sonarInput.frag), so a material's RESTITUTION IS ITS ACOUSTIC
    REFLECTIVITY. It is derived here, from R = (Z2 - Z1)/(Z2 + Z1) with
    Z_water = 1027 * 1482 = 1.522e6 Pa s/m:

        NetTwine   PA6      1140 * 2620 = 2.99e6    R = 0.33
        HDPE       collar    950 * 2430 = 2.31e6    R = 0.21
        FjordMud            1400 * 1500 = 2.10e6    R = 0.16
        Steel               7850 * 5900 = 4.63e7    R = 0.94  ->  CAPPED AT 0.85

    Steel is capped because 0.94 is an absurd coefficient of restitution for a mechanical
    contact and the barge and the anchors are things the vehicle can hit. under_ice.scn kept
    its acoustic value and accepted a lively bounce off a keel; here the trade goes the other
    way, because there is far more steel and the vehicle is far more likely to touch it.

    Salmon are 0.90, which is a deliberate lie. A salmon is bright on sonar because of its gas
    swimbladder, not its flesh - flesh alone would give R = 0.04. Stonefish has one number per
    material and the swimbladder is what a real echo sees, so the swimbladder wins. It does not
    matter for collisions: the fish are non-colliding.

    ============================ KNOWN LIMITATIONS ============================
    - A Terrain has ONE material, so the fjord wall is acoustically mud and the gassy,
      acoustically bright deposition footprint under the pens exists only in the albedo. A
      separate patch terrain was rejected: the seam is worse than the omission.
    - <look uv_scale> is read for every static body but only PASSED ON for planes and terrain
      (ScenarioParser.cpp:1097 and :1112). Model UVs must be baked into the .obj.
    - The seabed look is textured, and that is load-bearing for more than appearance:
      OpenGLContent::LoadTexture sets stbi_set_flip_vertically_on_load(true) and nothing ever
      resets it, so whether the heightmap loads bottom-up depends on a texture having been
      loaded first. <looks> is parsed before <static>, so it holds. If this look were ever
      reduced to a flat colour the seabed would silently mirror in y.
    - The net does not respond to the current, the collar does not heave, and the pens do not
      rotate on their moorings. See THE NET IS FROZEN above.
-->
<scenario>
	<solver>
		<multithreading max_physics_threads="8"/>
	</solver>

	<environment>
		<ned latitude="63.5" longitude="8.5"/>

		<ocean>
			<!-- Jerlov 0.30 is moderately turbid coastal water, roughly 10 m of visibility.
			     A working salmon site in autumn is murkier than this; it is set where it is
			     because the subject of the scene is 20 m across and has to be findable. -->
			<water density="1026.0" jerlov="0.30" temperature="8.0"/>
			<waves height="0.0"/>
			<particles enabled="true"/>
			<!-- The design current. THE NET IS BAKED AT THIS VALUE - see the header. -->
			<current type="uniform">
				<velocity xyz="0.0 0.30 0.0"/>
			</current>
		</ocean>

		<atmosphere>
			<sun azimuth="150.0" elevation="25.0"/>
			<conditions temperature="9.0" pressure="101000.0" humidity="0.80"/>
		</atmosphere>
	</environment>

	<materials>
		<!-- Neutral is required by the included vehicle, and physical materials can only be
		     defined in the MAIN file, never in an included one. -->
		<material name="Neutral" density="1000.0" restitution="0.5"/>
		<material name="NetTwine" density="1140.0" restitution="0.33"/>
		<material name="HDPE" density="950.0" restitution="0.21"/>
		<material name="Steel" density="7850.0" restitution="0.85"/>
		<material name="FjordMud" density="1400.0" restitution="0.16"/>
		<material name="MooringRope" density="1140.0" restitution="0.30"/>
		<material name="Salmon" density="1050.0" restitution="0.90"/>
		<!-- A foam-filled surface buoy: it has to float high, so its density is that of the
		     whole buoy and not of its plastic skin. 0.75 for the acoustic reflectivity of a
		     closed-cell foam float, which is nearly an air target. -->
		<material name="BuoyFoam" density="180.0" restitution="0.75"/>
		<friction_table>
			<!-- Unlisted pairs default to 1.0/1.0 (MaterialManager.cpp:64), which is far too
			     grippy for anything the vehicle touches, so every pair involving Neutral is
			     listed. Wet nylon netting on a plastic hull is genuinely slippery. -->
			<friction material1="Neutral" material2="Neutral" static="0.5" dynamic="0.2"/>
			<friction material1="Neutral" material2="NetTwine" static="0.15" dynamic="0.10"/>
			<friction material1="Neutral" material2="HDPE" static="0.25" dynamic="0.15"/>
			<friction material1="Neutral" material2="Steel" static="0.30" dynamic="0.20"/>
			<friction material1="Neutral" material2="FjordMud" static="0.50" dynamic="0.30"/>
			<friction material1="Neutral" material2="MooringRope" static="0.30" dynamic="0.20"/>
			<friction material1="Neutral" material2="Salmon" static="0.10" dynamic="0.05"/>
			<friction material1="Neutral" material2="BuoyFoam" static="0.30" dynamic="0.20"/>
			<friction material1="Steel" material2="FjordMud" static="0.60" dynamic="0.40"/>
			<friction material1="HDPE" material2="FjordMud" static="0.40" dynamic="0.25"/>
			<friction material1="MooringRope" material2="FjordMud" static="0.60" dynamic="0.40"/>
			<friction material1="FjordMud" material2="FjordMud" static="0.90" dynamic="0.70"/>
		</friction_table>
	</materials>

	<looks>
		<!-- The site albedo maps ONCE over the whole 400 m at uv_scale 1.0, which is what
		     carries the deposition footprint. The price is that there is no tiling normal map
		     for the seabed - a look has one uv_scale shared by both textures - so everything
		     finer than the 1.5 m height field is smooth-shaded. The footprint is the point of
		     this seabed, so it wins. under_ice.scn made the opposite trade. -->
		<look name="seabed" gray="1.0" roughness="0.95" texture="farm/seabed_albedo.png"/>
		<look name="net_clean" rgb="0.30 0.33 0.31" roughness="0.75"/>
		<look name="net_fouled" rgb="0.22 0.24 0.14" roughness="0.92"/>
		<look name="hdpe_black" rgb="0.06 0.06 0.07" roughness="0.55" reflectivity="0.04"/>
		<look name="hdpe_orange" rgb="0.75 0.30 0.05" roughness="0.55"/>
		<look name="steel_paint" rgb="0.55 0.56 0.58" roughness="0.45" metalness="0.6"/>
		<look name="rope" gray="1.0" roughness="0.90" texture="rope_color.jpg" normal_map="rope_normal.png"/>
		<!-- The counter-shading is the texture's whole job: a salmon reads as a fish because
		     of its dark back, silver flank and white belly, and a flat colour reads as a
		     grey lozenge. Roughness is low because a wet fish is glossy. -->
		<look name="salmon" gray="1.0" roughness="0.35" reflectivity="0.06" texture="farm/salmon.png"/>
	</looks>

	<!-- ================================ THE SEABED ================================
	     scalex/scaley/height and the world z all come from make_fish_farm.py; paste them
	     again after re-running it, do not hand-edit them. BLACK is DEEPEST here, which is the
	     opposite of the ice canopy's convention because this terrain is not flipped. -->
	<static name="Seabed" type="terrain">
		<height_map filename="farm/bathymetry_16b.png"/>
		<dimensions scalex="1.49813" scaley="1.49813" height="67.3399"/>
		<material name="FjordMud"/>
		<look name="seabed" uv_scale="1.0"/>
		<world_transform xyz="0.0 0.0 70.6915" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>


	<!-- ================================ THE HERO PEN ================================
	     Four objects, not sixteen panels - see THE PEN in the header. Every one carries a
	     twine mesh for rendering and a smooth shell for physics; without the shell the
	     vehicle flies between the twines and every net-profiler ray misses through a mesh
	     opening. Inside <physical> and <visual> the <origin> is read as the <mesh> element's
	     NEXT SIBLING, so that order is not optional.

	     The meshes are generated in pen-local x/y with ABSOLUTE z, and already carry the
	     baked downstream deflection, so the world transform is a pure translation. -->
	<static name="NetWallFouled" type="model">
		<physical>
			<mesh filename="farm/net_wall_fouled_phy.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</physical>
		<visual>
			<mesh filename="farm/net_wall_fouled_gra.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</visual>
		<material name="NetTwine"/>
		<look name="net_fouled"/>
		<world_transform xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>

	<static name="NetWallClean" type="model">
		<physical>
			<mesh filename="farm/net_wall_clean_phy.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</physical>
		<visual>
			<mesh filename="farm/net_wall_clean_gra.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</visual>
		<material name="NetTwine"/>
		<look name="net_clean"/>
		<world_transform xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>

	<!-- The torn sector. Its SHELL carries the same hole as its twine, so the vehicle can
	     fly through the tear and the net profiler loses its return over it. Both holes come
	     from one parameter in make_fish_farm.py; they cannot drift apart. -->
	<static name="NetWallTorn" type="model">
		<physical>
			<mesh filename="farm/net_wall_torn_phy.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</physical>
		<visual>
			<mesh filename="farm/net_wall_torn_gra.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</visual>
		<material name="NetTwine"/>
		<look name="net_clean"/>
		<world_transform xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>

	<static name="NetCone" type="model">
		<physical>
			<mesh filename="farm/net_cone_phy.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</physical>
		<visual>
			<mesh filename="farm/net_cone_gra.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</visual>
		<material name="NetTwine"/>
		<look name="net_clean"/>
		<world_transform xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>

	<!-- ============================== THE NEIGHBOUR PENS ==============================
	     70 m away, which is far beyond the visibility of this water, so they exist for the
	     surface camera and for the sonar. Coarse netting at a 1.0 m pitch: a deliberate,
	     documented level of detail, not an oversight. -->
	<static name="NetSouthWall" type="model">
		<physical>
			<mesh filename="farm/net_lod_wall_phy.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</physical>
		<visual>
			<mesh filename="farm/net_lod_wall_gra.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</visual>
		<material name="NetTwine"/>
		<look name="net_clean"/>
		<world_transform xyz="0.0 -70.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>

	<static name="NetSouthCone" type="model">
		<physical>
			<mesh filename="farm/net_lod_cone_phy.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</physical>
		<visual>
			<mesh filename="farm/net_lod_cone_gra.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</visual>
		<material name="NetTwine"/>
		<look name="net_clean"/>
		<world_transform xyz="0.0 -70.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>

	<static name="NetNorthWall" type="model">
		<physical>
			<mesh filename="farm/net_lod_wall_phy.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</physical>
		<visual>
			<mesh filename="farm/net_lod_wall_gra.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</visual>
		<material name="NetTwine"/>
		<look name="net_clean"/>
		<world_transform xyz="0.0 70.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>

	<static name="NetNorthCone" type="model">
		<physical>
			<mesh filename="farm/net_lod_cone_phy.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</physical>
		<visual>
			<mesh filename="farm/net_lod_cone_gra.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</visual>
		<material name="NetTwine"/>
		<look name="net_clean"/>
		<world_transform xyz="0.0 70.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>

	<!-- ============================== THE PEN STRUCTURE ==============================
	     The collar is what makes the site read as a farm from the surface, and it is the
	     scene's main test of the above-water path: twin 400 mm HDPE tubes centred 60 mm
	     PROUD of the waterline, with stanchions and a handrail 0.9 m above it. Its physics
	     is the two tubes only, at a quarter of the resolution - nothing else is reachable
	     by a vehicle that has to stay below the surface.

	     The sinker tube, the lifting straps and the cone ropes all carry the same baked
	     downstream deflection as the net, so the bottom ring is 1.7 m out of plumb and the
	     whole lower net leans with it. That offset IS the visible signature of the current. -->
	<static name="CollarHero" type="model">
		<physical>
			<mesh filename="farm/collar_phy.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</physical>
		<visual>
			<mesh filename="farm/collar.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</visual>
		<material name="HDPE"/>
		<look name="hdpe_black"/>
		<world_transform xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>
	<static name="SinkerTubeHero" type="model">
		<physical>
			<mesh filename="farm/sinker_tube.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</physical>
		<material name="HDPE"/>
		<look name="hdpe_black"/>
		<world_transform xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>

	<static name="CollarSouth" type="model">
		<physical>
			<mesh filename="farm/collar_phy.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</physical>
		<visual>
			<mesh filename="farm/collar.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</visual>
		<material name="HDPE"/>
		<look name="hdpe_black"/>
		<world_transform xyz="0.0 -70.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>
	<static name="SinkerTubeSouth" type="model">
		<physical>
			<mesh filename="farm/sinker_tube.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</physical>
		<material name="HDPE"/>
		<look name="hdpe_black"/>
		<world_transform xyz="0.0 -70.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>

	<static name="CollarNorth" type="model">
		<physical>
			<mesh filename="farm/collar_phy.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</physical>
		<visual>
			<mesh filename="farm/collar.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</visual>
		<material name="HDPE"/>
		<look name="hdpe_black"/>
		<world_transform xyz="0.0 70.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>
	<static name="SinkerTubeNorth" type="model">
		<physical>
			<mesh filename="farm/sinker_tube.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</physical>
		<material name="HDPE"/>
		<look name="hdpe_black"/>
		<world_transform xyz="0.0 70.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>

	<static name="PenRopes" type="model">
		<physical>
			<mesh filename="farm/pen_ropes.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</physical>
		<material name="MooringRope"/>
		<look name="rope"/>
		<world_transform xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>

	<!-- Mortalities settle into this cone and are airlifted up the lift-up pipe, which is
	     the only structure crossing the middle of the pen. Both are things an ROV is
	     actually sent down to look at. -->
	<static name="Mortality" type="model">
		<physical>
			<mesh filename="farm/mortality.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</physical>
		<material name="HDPE"/>
		<look name="hdpe_black"/>
		<world_transform xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>

	<!-- ============================== THE FARM ==============================
	     The feed barge straddles the waterline by design - 2.5 m of draft and 2.0 m of
	     freeboard - which makes it the scene's clearest check that above-water geometry
	     renders correctly rather than blowing out white. -->
	<static name="FeedBarge" type="model">
		<physical>
			<mesh filename="farm/barge_phy.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</physical>
		<visual>
			<mesh filename="farm/barge.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</visual>
		<material name="Steel"/>
		<look name="steel_paint"/>
		<world_transform xyz="0.0 -145.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>

	<static name="FeedHose" type="model">
		<physical>
			<mesh filename="farm/feed_hose.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</physical>
		<material name="HDPE"/>
		<look name="hdpe_black"/>
		<world_transform xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>

	<!-- The mooring frame at 10 m and the eight dead anchor lines. Four more lines are
	     live <cable> soft bodies further down this file: they hang in a real catenary and
	     lean with the current, and they are the four the vehicle flies past. A CableEntity
	     is solved every physics step, so four is a demonstration and forty is a stall. -->
	<static name="MooringFrame" type="model">
		<physical>
			<mesh filename="farm/moorings.obj" scale="1.0"/>
			<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		</physical>
		<material name="MooringRope"/>
		<look name="rope"/>
		<world_transform xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>

	<!-- ============================ THE MOORING ============================
	     Anchor positions and depths come from make_fish_farm.py, which samples its own
	     bathymetry, so an anchor cannot end up buried or hanging in mid-water. Paste them
	     again after re-running the generator. -->
	<static name="Anchor0" type="box">
		<dimensions xyz="2.6 2.6 1.2"/>
		<material name="Steel"/><look name="steel_paint" uv_mode="2"/>
		<world_transform xyz="-97.6 -168.6 63.14" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>
	<static name="Anchor1" type="box">
		<dimensions xyz="2.6 2.6 1.2"/>
		<material name="Steel"/><look name="steel_paint" uv_mode="2"/>
		<world_transform xyz="97.6 -168.6 68.29" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>
	<static name="Anchor2" type="box">
		<dimensions xyz="2.6 2.6 1.2"/>
		<material name="Steel"/><look name="steel_paint" uv_mode="2"/>
		<world_transform xyz="-124.0 -35.0 62.10" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>
	<static name="Anchor3" type="box">
		<dimensions xyz="2.6 2.6 1.2"/>
		<material name="Steel"/><look name="steel_paint" uv_mode="2"/>
		<world_transform xyz="124.0 -35.0 69.01" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>
	<static name="Anchor4" type="box">
		<dimensions xyz="2.6 2.6 1.2"/>
		<material name="Steel"/><look name="steel_paint" uv_mode="2"/>
		<world_transform xyz="-124.0 35.0 58.06" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>
	<static name="Anchor5" type="box">
		<dimensions xyz="2.6 2.6 1.2"/>
		<material name="Steel"/><look name="steel_paint" uv_mode="2"/>
		<world_transform xyz="124.0 35.0 69.21" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>
	<static name="Anchor6" type="box">
		<dimensions xyz="2.6 2.6 1.2"/>
		<material name="Steel"/><look name="steel_paint" uv_mode="2"/>
		<world_transform xyz="-97.6 168.6 63.51" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>
	<static name="Anchor7" type="box">
		<dimensions xyz="2.6 2.6 1.2"/>
		<material name="Steel"/><look name="steel_paint" uv_mode="2"/>
		<world_transform xyz="97.6 168.6 67.78" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>

	<cable name="AnchorLine2" physics="submerged" buoyant="true">
		<geometry diameter="0.048" number_of_segments="40"/>
		<material name="MooringRope" stretch_factor="0.02"/>
		<look name="rope" uv_scale="20.0"/>
		<first_end position="-124.0 -35.0 62.30" anchor="world"/>
		<second_end position="-34.0 -35.0 10.00" anchor="world"/>
	</cable>
	<cable name="AnchorLine3" physics="submerged" buoyant="true">
		<geometry diameter="0.048" number_of_segments="40"/>
		<material name="MooringRope" stretch_factor="0.02"/>
		<look name="rope" uv_scale="20.0"/>
		<first_end position="124.0 -35.0 69.21" anchor="world"/>
		<second_end position="34.0 -35.0 10.00" anchor="world"/>
	</cable>
	<cable name="AnchorLine4" physics="submerged" buoyant="true">
		<geometry diameter="0.048" number_of_segments="40"/>
		<material name="MooringRope" stretch_factor="0.02"/>
		<look name="rope" uv_scale="20.0"/>
		<first_end position="-124.0 35.0 58.26" anchor="world"/>
		<second_end position="-34.0 35.0 10.00" anchor="world"/>
	</cable>
	<cable name="AnchorLine5" physics="submerged" buoyant="true">
		<geometry diameter="0.048" number_of_segments="40"/>
		<material name="MooringRope" stretch_factor="0.02"/>
		<look name="rope" uv_scale="20.0"/>
		<first_end position="124.0 35.0 69.41" anchor="world"/>
		<second_end position="34.0 35.0 10.00" anchor="world"/>
	</cable>

	<dynamic name="Buoy0" type="sphere" physics="floating" buoyant="true">
		<dimensions radius="0.70"/>
		<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		<material name="BuoyFoam"/><look name="hdpe_orange"/>
		<world_transform xyz="-34.0 -105.0 -0.35" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</dynamic>
	<cable name="Pennant0" physics="submerged" buoyant="false">
		<geometry diameter="0.040" number_of_segments="8"/>
		<material name="MooringRope" stretch_factor="0.02"/>
		<look name="rope" uv_scale="6.0"/>
		<first_end position="-34.0 -105.0 10.00" anchor="world"/>
		<second_end position="-34.0 -105.0 -0.35" anchor="dynamic">
			<body name="Buoy0"/>
		</second_end>
	</cable>
	<dynamic name="Buoy1" type="sphere" physics="floating" buoyant="true">
		<dimensions radius="0.70"/>
		<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		<material name="BuoyFoam"/><look name="hdpe_orange"/>
		<world_transform xyz="34.0 -105.0 -0.35" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</dynamic>
	<cable name="Pennant1" physics="submerged" buoyant="false">
		<geometry diameter="0.040" number_of_segments="8"/>
		<material name="MooringRope" stretch_factor="0.02"/>
		<look name="rope" uv_scale="6.0"/>
		<first_end position="34.0 -105.0 10.00" anchor="world"/>
		<second_end position="34.0 -105.0 -0.35" anchor="dynamic">
			<body name="Buoy1"/>
		</second_end>
	</cable>
	<dynamic name="Buoy2" type="sphere" physics="floating" buoyant="true">
		<dimensions radius="0.70"/>
		<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		<material name="BuoyFoam"/><look name="hdpe_orange"/>
		<world_transform xyz="-34.0 -35.0 -0.35" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</dynamic>
	<cable name="Pennant2" physics="submerged" buoyant="false">
		<geometry diameter="0.040" number_of_segments="8"/>
		<material name="MooringRope" stretch_factor="0.02"/>
		<look name="rope" uv_scale="6.0"/>
		<first_end position="-34.0 -35.0 10.00" anchor="world"/>
		<second_end position="-34.0 -35.0 -0.35" anchor="dynamic">
			<body name="Buoy2"/>
		</second_end>
	</cable>
	<dynamic name="Buoy3" type="sphere" physics="floating" buoyant="true">
		<dimensions radius="0.70"/>
		<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		<material name="BuoyFoam"/><look name="hdpe_orange"/>
		<world_transform xyz="34.0 -35.0 -0.35" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</dynamic>
	<cable name="Pennant3" physics="submerged" buoyant="false">
		<geometry diameter="0.040" number_of_segments="8"/>
		<material name="MooringRope" stretch_factor="0.02"/>
		<look name="rope" uv_scale="6.0"/>
		<first_end position="34.0 -35.0 10.00" anchor="world"/>
		<second_end position="34.0 -35.0 -0.35" anchor="dynamic">
			<body name="Buoy3"/>
		</second_end>
	</cable>
	<dynamic name="Buoy4" type="sphere" physics="floating" buoyant="true">
		<dimensions radius="0.70"/>
		<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		<material name="BuoyFoam"/><look name="hdpe_orange"/>
		<world_transform xyz="-34.0 35.0 -0.35" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</dynamic>
	<cable name="Pennant4" physics="submerged" buoyant="false">
		<geometry diameter="0.040" number_of_segments="8"/>
		<material name="MooringRope" stretch_factor="0.02"/>
		<look name="rope" uv_scale="6.0"/>
		<first_end position="-34.0 35.0 10.00" anchor="world"/>
		<second_end position="-34.0 35.0 -0.35" anchor="dynamic">
			<body name="Buoy4"/>
		</second_end>
	</cable>
	<dynamic name="Buoy5" type="sphere" physics="floating" buoyant="true">
		<dimensions radius="0.70"/>
		<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		<material name="BuoyFoam"/><look name="hdpe_orange"/>
		<world_transform xyz="34.0 35.0 -0.35" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</dynamic>
	<cable name="Pennant5" physics="submerged" buoyant="false">
		<geometry diameter="0.040" number_of_segments="8"/>
		<material name="MooringRope" stretch_factor="0.02"/>
		<look name="rope" uv_scale="6.0"/>
		<first_end position="34.0 35.0 10.00" anchor="world"/>
		<second_end position="34.0 35.0 -0.35" anchor="dynamic">
			<body name="Buoy5"/>
		</second_end>
	</cable>
	<dynamic name="Buoy6" type="sphere" physics="floating" buoyant="true">
		<dimensions radius="0.70"/>
		<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		<material name="BuoyFoam"/><look name="hdpe_orange"/>
		<world_transform xyz="-34.0 105.0 -0.35" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</dynamic>
	<cable name="Pennant6" physics="submerged" buoyant="false">
		<geometry diameter="0.040" number_of_segments="8"/>
		<material name="MooringRope" stretch_factor="0.02"/>
		<look name="rope" uv_scale="6.0"/>
		<first_end position="-34.0 105.0 10.00" anchor="world"/>
		<second_end position="-34.0 105.0 -0.35" anchor="dynamic">
			<body name="Buoy6"/>
		</second_end>
	</cable>
	<dynamic name="Buoy7" type="sphere" physics="floating" buoyant="true">
		<dimensions radius="0.70"/>
		<origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
		<material name="BuoyFoam"/><look name="hdpe_orange"/>
		<world_transform xyz="34.0 105.0 -0.35" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</dynamic>
	<cable name="Pennant7" physics="submerged" buoyant="false">
		<geometry diameter="0.040" number_of_segments="8"/>
		<material name="MooringRope" stretch_factor="0.02"/>
		<look name="rope" uv_scale="6.0"/>
		<first_end position="34.0 105.0 10.00" anchor="world"/>
		<second_end position="34.0 105.0 -0.35" anchor="dynamic">
			<body name="Buoy7"/>
		</second_end>
	</cable>

	<!-- ============================== THE PEN LIGHTS ==============================
	     Submerged photoperiod LEDs, two of them at 9 m, hung inside the pen. They are real
	     equipment - blue-green light at depth suppresses early maturation, so every
	     Norwegian site runs them through the winter - and they are also what makes the
	     inside of the pen worth looking at.

	     POINT lights, deliberately. OpenGLPipeline bakes a shadowmap for every active light
	     each frame, but OpenGLLight::BakeShadowmap is a no-op and only OpenGLSpotLight
	     overrides it: a spot light therefore costs a whole extra pass over the scene and a
	     point light costs nothing. The vehicle's two Lumens already pay that twice. -->
	<light name="PenLight0">
		<specs illuminance="12000.0" radius="0.12"/>
		<color rgb="0.42 0.78 1.0"/>
		<world_transform xyz="6.36 6.36 9.00" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</light>

	<light name="PenLight1">
		<specs illuminance="12000.0" radius="0.12"/>
		<color rgb="0.42 0.78 1.0"/>
		<world_transform xyz="-6.36 6.36 9.00" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</light>


	<!-- ============================== THE VEHICLE ==============================
	     All three arguments are required: the parser only substitutes $(arg ...) when the
	     include passes at least one, and bluerov2_heavy.scn defines no defaults.
	     dvl_up="0" points the DVL at the seabed, 45 m below - out of range for altitude hold
	     over the pen, which is why this demo flies depth rather than altitude. -->
	<include file="bluerov2_heavy.scn">
		<arg name="robot_name" value="BLUEROV2"/>
		<arg name="robot_position" value="-21.6 0.0 3.0"/>
		<arg name="dvl_up" value="0"/>
	</include>
</scenario>
